 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-meaning"><h3>What is Kaffarah, and why does the kind of case matter?</h3>
  <p><em>Kaffarah</em> refers to expiation in specified circumstances. A deliberate binding oath, an ordinary promise, a fast missed through an exemption, accidental breaking of a fast and the act described in a particular Ramadan hadith may have <strong>different</strong> legal outcomes. You cannot identify the right path merely from the number of days or a cash amount.</p>
  <p>The two main sources used by this page concern separate events. The Quran directly addresses deliberate oaths in 5:89. The hadith in Bukhari 1936 describes one man's intercourse while fasting in Ramadan and his conversation with the Prophet ﷺ. They are not two price lists for the same obligation. Every selection in the calculator is an <strong>illustration conditional on outside guidance</strong>, never a finding of guilt, a binding expiation or a recommendation to pay a particular charity.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-oaths"><h3>Kaffarah for breaking an oath: feeding, clothing and fasting</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/5/89" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 5:89</a> distinguishes unintentional or meaningless expressions from deliberate oaths. It speaks of feeding ten needy people on the basis of ordinary family food, or clothing them, or freeing a slave. For those who do not have the means for the applicable listed alternatives, the verse mentions fasting for three days.</p>
  <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Feeding illustration</h4><p><strong>10 recipients per hypothetical distinct oath</strong>, if that remedy applies. Food amount and delivery rules need verification.</p></div><div><h4>Clothing illustration</h4><p><strong>10 recipients per hypothetical distinct oath</strong>. The qualifying clothing requirements need verification.</p></div><div><h4>Fasting alternative</h4><p><strong>Three days</strong> under the verse's inability condition. This is not a free choice to bypass an affordable prescribed remedy.</p></div></div>
  <p>A calculator cannot tell whether a particular statement was a binding oath, whether an oath was deliberately broken, if several statements create one or multiple liabilities, or whether a monetary substitute is accepted. Obtain a qualified determination on those issues first.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-ramadan"><h3>Kaffarah for a specific Ramadan fast violation: what does Bukhari 1936 report?</h3>
  <p>In <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1936" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1936</a>, a man reported having sexual intercourse while fasting in Ramadan. The narrated exchange asks whether he can free a slave, then whether he can fast <strong>two successive months</strong>, then whether he can feed <strong>sixty needy people</strong>. The same narration goes on to discuss the man's poverty and a basket of dates.</p>
  <p>That full context matters. The narration is <strong>not</strong> evidence that every missed fast automatically incurs feeding sixty people, that anyone may simply choose the cheapest option or that two successive <em>lunar</em> months always equal exactly sixty Gregorian days. The act, intention, exemption, ability, remedy order and relevant school of law all matter. Some schools differ on which Ramadan fast-breaking acts incur this type of expiation.</p>
  <p>For ordinary days to be made up, use the <a href="/tools/fasting-tracker/">Qada Tracker</a>. For feeding compensation in particular inability-to-fast circumstances, read the <a href="/tools/fidya-calculator/">Fidya guide</a>. Do not import a number from either as a Kaffarah obligation without qualified advice.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-steps"><h3>How to use a Kaffarah calculator without mistaking an estimate for a ruling</h3>
  <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li><strong>Ask which legal category applies.</strong> A broken deliberate oath is not the same as a specific Ramadan violation.</li><li><strong>Select the sourced case.</strong> The tool displays the relevant alternatives and cautions.</li><li><strong>Choose an illustrative remedy.</strong> Feeding, clothing or a fasting description may be shown, but no option is pronounced valid for you.</li><li><strong>For oaths, enter a reviewed number of distinct cases.</strong> Repeated statements are not necessarily independent liabilities.</li><li><strong>Supply a verified local rate if using a cost example.</strong> No assumed UK or universal amount is retrieved.</li><li><strong>Review the recipient count and arithmetic.</strong> A currency total is a conditional cost model, not permission for cash substitution.</li><li><strong>Export if useful.</strong> The text summary repeats that the case, remedy and delivery must be approved by a qualified authority.</li></ol>
  <p>There are no questions about your personal conduct, age, diagnosis or relationship details because an automated questionnaire would risk sounding like a legal verdict. Instead, the page gives the primary references so you can discuss your circumstances with a qualified scholar while keeping private information off the website.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-example"><h3>Worked examples: two oaths compared with one Ramadan feeding scenario</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-example"><p><strong>Hypothetical oath example:</strong> Suppose a qualified adviser has determined that <strong>two distinct oath cases</strong> independently call for feeding. Each uses ten recipients, giving <strong>20 recipient places</strong>. At an invented illustration of <strong>£7 per person</strong>, the arithmetic is <strong>20 × £7 = £140</strong>. It does not establish that two oaths are actually owed or that monetary payment is acceptable.</p><p><strong>Hypothetical Ramadan example:</strong> If the adviser has determined that feeding sixty people is the applicable remedy in one specific case, <strong>60 × £7 = £420</strong>. This does not mean all broken fasts cost £420, nor that the preceding remedies can automatically be skipped.</p></div>
  <p>Likewise, selecting a fasting alternative is not a declaration of eligibility for it. The tool presents the oath's three-day reference and the Ramadan report's two consecutive lunar months as distinct descriptions without converting months into an invented fixed-day obligation.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="kaffarah-privacy"><h3>Are financial details saved, and can you pay Kaffarah here?</h3>
  <p>No account, donation checkout, rate feed or automatic payment is attached to this calculator. Currency, scenario and per-person cost exist only in the active page state; the calculator does not write them to browser local storage or send them to a charity. The optional text download and browser Print / Save PDF produce files on your device at your request.</p>
  <p>If a charity quotes an amount, confirm whether it is for an oath, Fidya, Fitrana or a particular Ramadan expiation and what its distribution method is. Similar labels and prices do not make the obligations equivalent. Treat the arithmetic as a prompt for verification, not a completed donation or religious discharge.</p>
 </section>
